# Architecture

Use a modular monolith. Each feature lives in `{{ config.package }}.modules`
and contains the Python packages `routers`, `services`, and `repositories`.
Keep modules independent by default. `main.py` composes the application.
If present, `shared` must not depend on feature modules.

## Reference layout

The following is an illustrative `tree` output, not generated application
code. Replace `example` with a feature name and add modules as needed.
`shared` is optional.

```text
{{ config.source }}/
└── {{ config.package }}/
    ├── __init__.py
    ├── main.py
    ├── modules/
    │   ├── __init__.py
    │   └── example/
    │       ├── __init__.py
    │       ├── routers/
    │       │   ├── __init__.py
    │       │   └── router.py
    │       ├── services/
    │       │   ├── __init__.py
    │       │   └── example_service.py
    │       └── repositories/
    │           ├── __init__.py
    │           └── example_repository.py
    └── shared/
        └── __init__.py
```

## Boundaries

Dependencies flow from routers to services to repositories. Routers must not
import repositories directly. Keep HTTP handling in routers, business rules in
services, and database access in repositories. Do not introduce a hexagonal
architecture, generic base repositories, interfaces or factories without a
specific, documented need. Prefer the simplest design that solves the problem.

Register each module in `harness.yaml`. `fastapi-harness check` verifies the
package layout, static import boundaries, complexity, types and style; it
cannot detect every architectural violation or verify business behavior.
Document and review exceptions with the team rather than bypassing checks.
